  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev22-04",
    "question": "<p>The points A, B and C have position vectors <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left( \\begin{array}{c} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\end{array} \\right), \\left( \\begin{array}{c} - 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{array} \\right)\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left( \\begin{array}{c} 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{array} \\right)\"></span> respectively.</p><p>(a) Find a vector equation of the straight line AB. <span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>(b) Find a cartesian form of the equation of the straight line AB. <span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>The plane <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi\"></span> contains the points A, B and C.</p><p>(c) Find a vector equation of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi\"></span> in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{r}.\\mathbf{n}=p.\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></p><p>(d) Find the perpendicular distance from the origin to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi.\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>Subtract the same anchor point <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span> from <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span> to form two in-plane vectors. Their cross product is perpendicular to every direction in that plane. Use any of the three known points to evaluate the constant in its Cartesian equation.</p>",
      "<p>(a) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B-A=(-2,-3,-1)\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf r=(1,3,2)+t(-2,-3,-1)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) Eliminating <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(x-1)/2=(y-3)/3=z-2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(c) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C-A=(1,-2,-2)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The two direction vectors have cross product <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(4,-5,7)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>The point <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf r\\cdot(4,-5,7)=3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(d) From the origin the perpendicular distance is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"|0-3|/\\sqrt{16+25+49}=1/\\sqrt{10}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 10 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev22-04-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2015 FP3 · Q5, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev22-05",
    "question": "<p>The plane <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi_{1}\"></span> has vector equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{r}.\\left( \\begin{array}{c} 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\end{array} \\right)=5\"></span></p><p>The plane <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi_{2}\"></span> has vector equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{r}.\\left( \\begin{array}{c}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\end{array} \\right)=7\"></span></p><p>(a) Find a vector equation for the line of intersection of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi_{1}\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi_{2}\"></span> , giving your answer in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}\"></span> where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{a}\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{b}\"></span> are constant vectors and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\lambda\"></span> is a scalar parameter.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></p><p>The plane <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi_{3}\"></span> has cartesian equation</p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"x-y+2z=31\"></div><p>(b) Using your answer to part (a), or otherwise, find the coordinates of the point of intersection of the planes <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi_{1},\\varPi_{2}\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi_{3}\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The cross product of the normals gives the direction but does not locate the line. Setting <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z=4\"></span> then supplies a convenient actual point <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(-1,-5,4)\"></span>. Check it in both original equations before intersecting the line with the third plane.</p>",
      "<p>(a) A direction is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(2,1,3)\\times(-1,2,4)=(-2,-11,5)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>With <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z=4\"></span> the two planes give <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2x+y=-7,\\ -x+2y=-9\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=-1,y=-5\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The intersection line is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf r=(-1,-5,4)+t(-2,-11,5)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) Substitute into <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x-y+2z=31\"></span> to get <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"12+19t=31\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Hence <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=1\"></span>, giving <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(-3,-16,9)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 9 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev22-05-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2014 (R) FP3 · Q8, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev22-06",
    "question": "<p>The line <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"l\"></span> passes through the point <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P(2,1,3)\"></span> and is perpendicular to the plane <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi\"></span> whose vector equation is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{r}.(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})=3\"></span></p><p>Find</p><p>(a) a vector equation of the line <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"l,\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>(b) the position vector of the point where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"l\"></span> meets <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi.\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></p><p>(c) Hence find the perpendicular distance of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\"></span> from <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi.\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>Travelling in direction <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(1,-2,-1)\"></span> ensures that the connecting segment is perpendicular to the plane. Substitution determines the signed parameter; its magnitude alone is not the distance, since the direction vector has length <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sqrt6\"></span>. Multiply by that length.</p>",
      "<p>(a) A perpendicular line has direction <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(1,-2,-1)\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf r=(2,1,3)+t(1,-2,-1)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) The point has coordinates <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(2+t,1-2t,3-t)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Its dot product with the normal is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-3+6t=3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=1\"></span>, giving position vector <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(3,-1,2)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(c) The displacement from <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\"></span> is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(1,-2,-1)\"></span>, of length <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sqrt6\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 8 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev22-06-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2014 FP3 · Q1, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev22-07",
    "question": "<p>The plane <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi_{1}\"></span> has vector equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{r}.(3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})=5\"></span></p><p>(a) Find the perpendicular distance from the point (6, 2, 12) to the plane <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi_{1}\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></p><p>The plane <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi_{2}\"></span> has vector equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})+\\mu(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k})\"></span> , where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\lambda\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mu\"></span> are scalar parameters.</p><p>(b) Find the acute angle between <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi_{1}\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi_{2}\"></span> giving your answer to the nearest degree.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p><p>(c) Find an equation of the line of intersection of the two planes in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{r}\\times\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{b}\"></span> where a and b are constant vectors.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The expression <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3x-4y+2z-5\"></span> measures a signed displacement scaled by the normal length. Dividing by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sqrt{29}\"></span> converts its value into a physical distance. In the intersection calculation a direction vector must lie in both planes and hence be perpendicular to both normals.</p>",
      "<p>(a) The perpendicular distance is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"|3(6)-4(2)+2(12)-5|/\\sqrt{9+16+4}=29/\\sqrt{29}=\\sqrt{29}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[3]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) A normal to plane 2 is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(2,1,5)\\times(1,-1,-2)=(3,9,-3)\"></span>, proportional to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(1,3,-1)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Its dot product with <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(3,-4,2)\"></span> is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-11\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Thus the acute angle is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\arccos(11/\\sqrt{29\\cdot11})\\approx52^\\circ\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(c) Take the cross product of the normals to get a direction <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf a=(-2,5,13)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Setting <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"z=1\"></span> in <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3x-4y+2z=5,\\ x+3y-z=0\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(x,y)=(1,0)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf r\\times\\mathbf a=(1,0,1)\\times(-2,5,13)=(-5,-15,5)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 14 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev22-07-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2013 FP3 · Q8, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev22-07-ms-02.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2013 FP3 · Q8, extract 2\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev22-08",
    "question": "<p>Two skew lines <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"l_{1}\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"l_{2}\"></span> have equations</p><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"l_{1}\"></span> : <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{r}=(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})+\\lambda(4\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})\"></span></p><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"l_{2}\"></span> : <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{r}=(3\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})+\\mu(-4\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})\"></span></p><p>respectively, where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\lambda\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mu\"></span> are real parameters.</p><p>(a) Find a vector in the direction of the common perpendicular to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"l_{1}\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"l_{2}\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>(b) Find the shortest distance between these two lines.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>A common perpendicular is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(-3,-4,12)\"></span>, whose length is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"13\"></span>. The scalar product with a joining vector measures thirteen times its component along this perpendicular. Divide by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"13\"></span> and take the absolute value to obtain the shortest separation.</p>",
      "<p>(a) Cross the directions: <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(4,3,2)\\times(-4,6,1)=(-9,-12,36)\"></span>. A simpler parallel vector is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(-3,-4,12)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) Between the two given points the displacement is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(2,8,1)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Project onto the common perpendicular: the scalar product is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-6-32+12=-26\"></span> and the normal has length 13. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>The shortest distance is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"|-26|/13=2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 7 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev22-08-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2013 (R) FP3 · Q2, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev22-09",
    "question": "<p>The plane <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\"></span> has equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{r}=\\left( \\begin{array}{c} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\end{array} \\right)+\\lambda \\left( \\begin{array}{c} 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{array} \\right)+\\mu \\left( \\begin{array}{c} 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{array} \\right)\"></span></p><p>(a) Find a vector perpendicular to the plane <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\"></span>.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>The line <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"l\"></span> passes through the point <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span> (1, 3, 3) and meets <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\"></span> at (3, 1, 2).</p><p>The acute angle between the plane <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\"></span> and the line <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"l\"></span> is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\alpha\"></span>.</p><p>(b) Find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\alpha\"></span> to the nearest degree.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></p><p>(c) Find the perpendicular distance from <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span> to the plane <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\"></span>.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The line&#x27;s dot product with a unit normal measures its perpendicular component. If its angle to the plane is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\alpha\"></span>, the component is proportional to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sin\\alpha\"></span>, explaining the sine in part (b). A segment of length three therefore rises by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3(8/9)=8/3\"></span>.</p>",
      "<p>(a) A normal is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(0,2,-1)\\times(3,2,2)=(6,-3,-6)\"></span>; take <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(2,-1,-2)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) The line direction from <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span> is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(2,-2,-1)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The angle with the plane is complementary to the angle with its normal, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sin\\alpha=|4+2+2|/(3\\cdot3)=8/9\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\alpha\\approx63^\\circ\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(c) The distance from <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span> to the intersection is 3. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Its perpendicular component is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3\\sin\\alpha=8/3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[3]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 10 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev22-09-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2011 FP3 · Q6, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev22-10",
    "question": "<p>The plane <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi\"></span> has vector equation</p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{r}=3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}+\\lambda(-4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})+\\mu(6\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})\"></div><p>(a) Find an equation of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi\"></span> in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{r.n}=p\"></span> , where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf{n}\"></span> is a vector perpendicular to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi\"></span> and p is a constant.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p><p>The point P has coordinates (6,13,5). The line l passes through P and is perpendicular to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi\"></span> . The line l intersects <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi\"></span> at the point N.</p><p>(b) Show that the coordinates of N are (3,1,-1).</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></p><p>The point R lies on <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\varPi\"></span> and has coordinates (1,0,2).</p><p>(c) Find the perpendicular distance from N to the line PR. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The cross product <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(1,4,2)\"></span> has squared length <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"21\"></span>, so moving one parameter unit along it changes the plane expression by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"21\"></span>. For the final distance, treat <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"PR\"></span> as the base of a parallelogram with side <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"PN\"></span>. Its area divided by that base equals the required height.</p>",
      "<p>(a) A normal is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(-4,1,0)\\times(6,-2,1)=(1,4,2)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(3,0,1)\"></span> the scalar product is 5. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Hence <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathbf r\\cdot(1,4,2)=5\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) The line through <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\"></span> has points <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(6,13,5)+t(1,4,2)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Substitution in the plane gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"68+21t=5\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=-3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>This yields <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"N=(3,1,-1)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(c) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"PN=(-3,-12,-6)\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"PR=(-5,-13,-3)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The area of the parallelogram on these vectors is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"|PN\\times PR|=|(-42,21,-21)|=21\\sqrt6\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Divide by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"|PR|=\\sqrt{203}\"></span> to obtain the perpendicular distance <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"21\\sqrt{6/203}=3.61\"></span> (3 s.f.). <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>The distance can also be found by projection onto <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"PR\"></span>. From <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"PN\\cdot PR=189\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"|PR|^2=203\"></span>, so the squared distance off the line is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"189-189^2/203=2646/203\"></span>. Its square root is again <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"21\\sqrt{6/203}\"></span>.</p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 14 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev22-10-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2010 FP3 · Q7, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  }
